WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.680
Alright Pokémon HQ!

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.920
Let's take our most popular character,

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:05.680
recreate him in loving detail,

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.280
cast the Internet's favorite voice of sarcasm,

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:13.280
adhere to established canon and drop in a lot of Easter eggs to fans of this series.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.980
Movie, I choose you!!

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:18.960
Meanwhile, over at Sonic HQ!

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:20.680
Yeah, guys, let's do what they did,

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:22.680
but change everything about the character,

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.320
remove any ties to established lore,

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.780
and set the whole thing to classic gangster rap.

00:00:26.789 --> 00:00:28.789
Come on money...

00:00:29.769 --> 00:00:30.660
Hello internet!

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.040
Welcome to Film Theory!

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:38.380
The show that's kinda cringy when it tries to be funny, but at the end of the day is still endearing.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.240
Just like "Detective Pikachu"!

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.899
Hey, so based on the Internet's collective reaction,

00:00:42.899 --> 00:00:48.040
it seems like we finally got ourselves the first great video game movie of all time.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:51.000
"Don't overcook it. It's fun. It's good. It's enjoyable."

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.700
"It's about as good as a live-action Pokémon film could be."

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.300
"I'd probably say it's the first watchable video game movie."

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:04.120
OK, dialing back the praise here, the first watchable video game movie of all time.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:07.100
Now, in all honesty, I saw it last night and I have to agree.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.180
It was just a lot of fun.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:10.780
Nothing ground breaking,

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:13.420
but it was just a really enjoyable movie to watch.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:16.220
And, as an experience, it confirmed a lot of things for me...

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:18.120
Ryan Reynolds? Still funny.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.860
Pikachu? Still adorable.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.740
Mr. Mime? Still probably creeping on kids at night.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:23.920
But more so than any of that,

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.180
"Detective Pikachu" seems to have provided the final pieces of evidence

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:31.480
needed to confirm one of the longest-running and coolest Pokémon theories

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:35.580
that's been kicking around since the first generation of games way back in the 90s.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:39.200
In the process of solving the mystery of what happened to Tim Goodman's father,

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.520
Detective Pikachu may have also just solved a much more important mystery...

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:46.720
a case that's been open for over 20 years.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:52.160
A case whose solution has huge implications about the true nature of Pokémon.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:58.840
So pull out your magnifying glasses, and put on that deerstalker cap, because we've got ourselves a case to solve.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:02.520
Our case begins with the video games.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.160
Way back in 1998,

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.660
the year when everyone thought that they were king of the world,

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:12.280
and the height of mobile gaming was an 8-bit egg in your pocket named Tamagotchi.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:15.280
Little did we know then that we were in the middle of a revolution.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:17.260
The Pokémon revolution.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:18.200
Generation 1:

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.540
150 cute little pocket monsters

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:25.060
that would give rise to the highest-earning media franchise of all time.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:26.900
Eat your heart, out Hello Kitty!

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.960
2 years earlier in 1996,

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.900
the world had been met with Pokémon's "Red", "Green" and "Blue",

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:35.200
but then came "Pokémon Yellow" in 1998,

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.520
which contained one major change,

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.280
Pikachu was now your starter Pokémon,

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.199
and just followed you around the overworld.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.080
That was pretty much it.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:47.980
Other minor changes were to make it feel closer to the hugely popular anime series at the time.

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:50.580
The gym leader Giovanni using a Persian,

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:55.000
or characters like Jessie and James replacing generic Team Rocket grunt appearances.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.660
But other than that, it was just the original games all over again.

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:00.080
Or was it?

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:04.880
You see, there was one other very subtle change made in "Pokémon Yellow".

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.640
In the course of your journey across the Kanto region,

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.460
you visit a place called the Pokémon mansion on Cinnabar Island,

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:12.800
a massive building filled with rubble.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.880
Scattered throughout it are journal entries,

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:17.960
referencing the discovery of Mew in the jungles of Guyana,

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:22.800
and the subsequent creation of the ultra-powerful, ultra-violent Mewtwo.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.740
The wreckage around the mansion is the game's way of telling us that he was created here, and later escaped.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:29.500
Something that later games,

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:34.200
in particular the recent "Let's Go Pikachu!" and "Eevee" series makes a lot more explicit.

00:03:34.209 --> 00:03:38.180
You eventually find Mewtwo in the Cerulean cave, where he can finally be caught.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:40.320
But here's the important change.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.260
In "Pokémon Yellow", a new wild Pokémon can be found roaming the halls of the mansion:

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:47.440
Ditto, the transform Pokémon.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:53.600
A little blob of purple goo, unique for its ability to shape-shift using DNA, as his Pokédex entry says:

00:03:53.600 --> 00:04:01.240
"Capable of copying an enemy's genetic code, to instantly transform itself into a duplicate of the enemy."

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:04.029
Seems like it's just a minor shift in spawn locations, right?

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:05.040
Maybe.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:09.420
It seemed to many to be more than just a coincidence that a genetically based Pokémon

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:15.140
would appear in an area where Mewtwo was being created using genetic experimentation.

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:19.440
Perhaps there was a way that Ditto was connected to both Mew and Mewtwo.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.860
Maybe, just maybe, Dittos were the failed clones of Mew.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:25.780
The experiments that didn't make the cut,

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:30.300
until science was finally able to create the perfect Mew clone in Mewtwo.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:33.020
Seems like it's a bit of a stretch, right?

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:35.099
Theorists like me grasping at straws.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:37.700
All they did was change one spawn location.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:41.560
But the closer you looked into this thing, the more details started to line up.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:45.620
The connection between the two only got stronger when players noticed that,

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:46.680
(again, in "Yellow")

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:51.100
the only other spawn location for Ditto was the Cerulean Cave,

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:54.560
where Mewtwo winds up hiding out after his escape from the mansion.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.520
So wherever Mewtwo was, Dittos seemed to follow.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.120
It was enough to get theorists like me to start looking closer,

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:04.700
and boy howdy, did we ever find a treasure trove of evidence.

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:09.400
The similarities between Mew and Ditto are undeniable in the games.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:11.600
They have the exact same weight of 8.8 pounds,

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:16.360
they're the only two Pokémon that are able to learn the transform move purely by levelling up,

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:18.900
even their coloration seems to match.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:22.980
I mean, sure, Ditto is usually purple, and Mew is usually pink,

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:26.860
but sometimes, in some iterations of the games, Mew is purple.

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:30.460
And sometimes, in some iterations of the games, Ditto is pink.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:34.500
And in "Detective Pikachu" the Ditto was definitely much more in the pink spectrum.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:40.100
But there is no denying that all the time both Ditto and Mew have shiny forms,

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.500
that are both the same shade of light blue.

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:44.430
And this is all just the superficial stuff.

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:49.709
If you dig further into the game's data, even the numbers tell the story of how they're connected.

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:55.229
Each Pokemon has base stats that govern their basic attributes like HP, attack, defense, speed, etc.

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:59.069
Mu is a regular jack-of-all-trades when it comes to his base stats.

00:05:59.069 --> 00:06:01.709
He has equal base stats across the board.

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:06.280
If you look at Ditto's base stats, you'll find the exact same thing;

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:09.900
identical base stat distribution across the board.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:12.760
Now all of Ditto's are 52% lower than Mew's,

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:17.500
but it's exactly 52% for all six of the main stats.

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:21.080
The distribution of those statistics is identical.

00:06:21.089 --> 00:06:26.540
It's almost as if Ditto were a weaker copy of the original Mew, and that there's the point.

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:27.509
Ditto isn't Mew,

00:06:27.509 --> 00:06:28.740
it's an imperfect copy.

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:31.600
It's the result of failed cloning experiments,

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:34.120
cloning experiments that eventually give rise to Mewtwo,

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:39.260
and leave the little pile of gooey DNA Ditto over there in the corner, wandering around, looking for stuff to do.

00:06:39.449 --> 00:06:40.420
Kind of a weird Pokemon.

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:43.919
All of it adds up to a very compelling amount of circumstantial evidence.

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:47.920
But for the two decades since Yellow's release, that's all it's been.

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:48.960
Circumstantial.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:54.160
A bunch of similarities never outright confirmed by any singular piece of Pokemon media.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:56.880
And then came Detective Pikachu.

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:58.260
Let's lay out what we learned from the movie.

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:00.719
And yes, we're gonna have to have some minor spoilers here.

00:07:00.719 --> 00:07:03.569
Not the big twist of what happened to Tim's father or anything like that,

00:07:03.569 --> 00:07:06.280
But for the smaller mysteries that are uncovered along the way.

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:09.179
I'll give you three seconds, so you can use that time to click away,

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:14.320
and/or click the subscribe button to get notified of every time we have a cool theory, like this one!

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:17.100
Help this channel get to 10 million subscribers, please.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:19.080
It would be great to get that glorified paperweight.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:20.560
Alright, we all good?

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:22.000
Everyone ashore who's gone ashore?

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:23.060
Fantastic.

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:28.580
Within the movie, it's mentioned that Mewtwo is a genetically altered clone of the ancient Pokémon Mew,

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:30.640
confirming everything that we just talked about.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:31.919
But the movie goes one step further.

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:37.799
They explicitly say that Mewtwo disappeared from the Kanto region 20 years ago.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:41.480
Now the Kanto region is where the first games took place, and where the anime began.

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:43.700
Interesting coincidence, but nothing earth-shattering.

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:46.700
It's that "20 years" detail that really matters.

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:52.560
You see "Detective Pikachu" is coming out just over 20 years after the release of the Generation 1 games,

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:57.760
and exactly 20 years after the release of "Pokémon: Mewtwo Strikes Back",

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:02.840
the first ever Pokémon movie, which was all about, you guessed it, Mewtwo's escape.

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:04.400
It's all connected.

00:08:04.409 --> 00:08:06.420
And that's not even speculation,

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:11.000
it is something outright confirmed by the screenwriters, in an interview they did with Polygon.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:19.520
"The implication, which the screenwriters confirm to Polygon, is that this is the same Mewtwo from the canon universe."

00:08:19.529 --> 00:08:22.440
I mean, I gotta quibble with them saying "the canon universe",

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:25.229
because it's like, "Which canon universe are you talking about?".

00:08:25.229 --> 00:08:26.310
But hey, I will take it.

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:33.060
So we have an established and confirmed canonical connection between "Detective Pikachu" and the wider "Pokémon" franchise, cool!

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:35.780
But the writers went on in another interview,

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:36.919
this time with ScreenRant,

00:08:37.300 --> 00:08:42.060
to confirm that they were aware of the Ditto/Mew theories when working on the project.

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:46.520
"So we went into the history of Ditto, we looked at all of those things,"

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:50.840
"and we kind of wanted to leave it up to the audience of people that are more familiar [with the story]."

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:53.960
"We didn't necessarily feel that we wanted to go too much into that."

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:56.060
"But all of those things are a factor,"

00:08:56.060 --> 00:09:01.080
"and I think the deep origin of Ditto is a provocative question that we wanted to hint at."

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:04.640
"Because of the interaction with Mewtwo's genetic material,

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:09.520
"it did seem like there was a sensible connection between Mewtwo and the Ditto."

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:13.200
So, we have writers who've been approved to work within the official canon,

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:16.720
who were also aware of the possible connection between Ditto and Mew,

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:20.990
So now the question is: how does that actually translate to what we see on screen?

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:24.460
Well in the movie, we see Ditto playing a pivotal role.

00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:27.120
By the end of the film, it's revealed that Howard Clifford,

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:29.840
the founder of Ryme City, is our true villain.

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:35.300
A man who's out to hijack Mewtwo's mind, so he can gain the psychic Pokémon's powers for himself.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:38.989
In order to do this, he uses his partner Pokémon, Ditto.

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:41.960
But this isn't any ordinary Ditto.

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:45.540
You see Howard's Ditto has the ability to transform at will,

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:48.440
and in the film's climax, we see him do exactly that.

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:50.680
He transforms into a Machamp to beat up Tim,

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:54.520
then transforming into a Cubone to give him a few knuckle taps to knock him off a building,

00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:57.340
and later transforming into a raging Charizard.

00:09:57.340 --> 00:10:02.480
Heck, his Ditto can even transform into humans, which is a whole other discussion for another day.

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:08.080
All of these behaviors though, directly conflict with what we know about a normal Ditto's abilities.

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:13.720
We're told repeatedly throughout the animes and video games that Ditto can only copy things it sees.

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:18.200
In gameplay terms, this means that Ditto can't just transform into any random Pokemon,

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:21.020
it always has to copy whatever enemy it's facing.

00:10:21.020 --> 00:10:23.179
But this isn't just a gameplay mechanic.

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:26.140
It is something that's a part of this wider franchise's canon,

00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:28.790
as established via various Pokédex entries;

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:32.680
Ruby and Sapphire, Emerald, Diamond and Pearl, Black and White.

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:37.320
"Ditto rearranges it's cell structure to transform itself into other shapes."

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:44.960
"However, if it tries to transform itself into something by relying on it's memory, this Pokémon manages to get details wrong."

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:49.000
"A Ditto rearranges it's cell structure to transform itself."

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:53.100
"However, if it tries to change based on it's memory it will get details wrong."

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:59.320
"It has the ability to reconstitute it's entire cellular structure to transform into whatever it sees."

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:02.300
This is further supported by what we see in the anime.

00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:06.480
The first Ditto we meet in the anime are the ones belonging to the copycat Duplica,

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:07.840
and in her episodes,

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:12.120
we learned that Ditto are only capable of transforming into Pokémon that are physically present,

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:15.800
and that this is a limitation of the species as a whole.

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:18.200
Likewise all the other episodes that have Ditto,

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:22.018
only show Ditto transforming into Pokémon that are present for it to copy.

00:11:22.018 --> 00:11:23.380
Across all of our research,

00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:27.540
there were zero canonical cases of Ditto randomly shape-shifting at will,

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:30.140
without having a Pokémon nearby to copy.

00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:36.440
So for Howard's Ditto in "Detective Pikachu" to cycle between forms so rapidly and so precisely,

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:40.240
forms that it's not seeing, and is still able to replicate flawlessly,

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:43.280
it must be some kind of Super-Ditto.

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:46.500
Something that we haven't seen in this series before.

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:52.220
Something that was artificially birthed out of a laboratory off of Mewtwo's genetics.

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:56.180
Remember that, all this time, the working theory that we've been trying to prove,

00:11:56.180 --> 00:11:58.500
is the connection between Mewtwo and Ditto.

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:03.200
That, like Mewtwo, Ditto was also born out of the same genetic testing.

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:09.100
Now, in "Detective Pikachu", twenty years later, we see Ditto and Mewtwo connected yet again.

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:13.340
Howard Clifford is doing genetic experiments on Mewtwo on the outskirts of Ryme City,

00:12:13.340 --> 00:12:20.000
and in the process, produces a Ditto with powers that surpass the other members of its species.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:22.060
Tim says, "What kind of Ditto was that?"

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:24.459
"That was one of my father's genetic experiments."

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:30.100
If you were looking for the final piece of confirmation for the link between Mew, Mewtwo and Ditto, this is it.

00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:32.840
And probably as explicit as we're ever gonna get.

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:38.260
Ditto is made out of the genetic stuff that Mew and Mewtwo are also made out of.

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:39.860
It is more possible now than ever,

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:44.400
that Ditto is just a bunch of failed protoplasm coming out of Mew cloning experiments.

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:47.900
Back in 1998, it was all about creating the first forms of Mewtwo,

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:51.600
and the random regular Dittos that spawned out of that process.

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:55.580
Now twenty years later, in 2019 with "Detective Pikachu",

00:12:55.630 --> 00:13:01.299
it's elevating and exploiting Mewtwo's powers for the next level of Howard Clifford's evolution,

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:06.280
and in the process, he's creating a stronger, more advanced form of Ditto.

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:12.540
In the end, it took a Film Theory to confirm one of the most famous Game Theories of all time.

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:15.560
But hey, that's just a theory!

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:18.800
A multimedia cross-platform theory!

